Q: What to include to access a standard concept, such as ContiguousContainer? A: <p>For most purposes, <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/79931605/2610810">Richards answer</a> is sufficient, but if you really want to test that you have a container, and not a view or similar, you can write a concept yourself.</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>template &lt;typename C&gt;
concept container = std::ranges::sized_range&lt;C&gt; 
  &amp;&amp; std::ranges::forward_range&lt;C&gt;
  &amp;&amp; std::regular&lt;C&gt;
  &amp;&amp; std::swappable&lt;C&gt;
  &amp;&amp; std::same_as&lt;typename C::reference, typename C::value_type &amp;&gt;
  &amp;&amp; std::same_as&lt;typename C::const_reference, const typename C::value_type &amp;&gt;;

template &lt;typename C&gt;
concept contiguous_container = container&lt;C&gt; &amp;&amp; std::ranges::contiguous_range&lt;C&gt;;
</code></pre>
<p>There is some slight laxity with using these concepts, as they check for <code>ranges::size(c)</code> rather than <code>c.size()</code> etc.</p>
